    "intro": "The ogre comes from fairy tales, where it is a huge, hungry giant who eats travellers, and from D&D, where ogres live in tribes and fight with clubs. This **ogre name generator** makes brutish names, fairy-tale ogres, D&D ogres with a tribe and funny ogres who mostly want lunch.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where ogres come from",
        "content": "<p>The word ogre first appears in French fairy tales, most famously in Charles Perrault's collection of 1697.</p><ul><li><strong>Perrault's ogres</strong> eat people, especially children: the ogre outwitted in \"Puss in Boots\" and the ogre in \"Hop-o'-My-Thumb\", whose seven-league boots are stolen.</li><li><strong>An ogress</strong> appears in Perrault's \"Sleeping Beauty\", as the prince's mother, who wants to eat her grandchildren.</li><li><strong>The origin</strong> of the word is uncertain. One suggestion is Orcus, a Roman god of the underworld.</li><li><strong>In D&amp;D,</strong> ogres are huge, greedy and live in tribes, which is why tribe names suit them.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Naming an ogre",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Brutish</strong> ogres get short, thick names: Grubnak, Smashra.</li><li><strong>Fairy-tale</strong> ogres get an old, local name: Crookshin of Thornwood.</li><li><strong>D&amp;D</strong> ogres get a tribe: Bolga of the Skull Rock.</li><li><strong>Funny</strong> ogres are friendlier than they look: Sir Snackalot, Little Nigel.</li></ul><p>For other big brutes, try the <a href=\"troll-name-generator.html\">Troll</a> and <a href=\"giant-name-generator.html\">Giant</a> name generators.</p><p>For more beasts of legend, see all the <a href=\"../categories/mythical-names.html\">mythical creature name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
